  "question": "Are AI exposure and remote-work feasibility separately identified in occupation-level data?",
  "why_it_matters": "Emanuel, Harrington & Pallais attribute 64% of the rise in young college-graduate unemployment to remote work. Brynjolfsson, Chandar & Chen control for interest-rate exposure but not telework. If the two measures overlap heavily, the attribution in that literature is not identified from occupation-level data.",
  "how_to_read": "corr_employment_weighted says how alike the measures are. off_diagonal_employment_share says how much employment sits in occupations where they DISAGREE -- that is the variation any decomposition is identified from. A high correlation with a thick off-diagonal is workable; a moderate correlation whose divergent cells hold little employment is not.",
  "inputs": {
    "dingel_neiman": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-research-portfolio/f3e36e66-2ee3-5ba1-be3f-3a113d2e18f2/scratchpad/exposure/dingel_neiman_occ.csv",
    "eloundou": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-research-portfolio/f3e36e66-2ee3-5ba1-be3f-3a113d2e18f2/scratchpad/exposure/eloundou_occ.csv",
